# The block this project adds to the shared Caddyfile at /opt/attendanceqr/Caddyfile on 94.20.153.137.
#
# IT LIVES HERE, IN THE REPO, ON PURPOSE. The catalogue's block once existed only on the server, typed in by
# hand — so when a cutover replaced that Caddyfile, the block vanished with it and the site went dark until
# somebody noticed. That Caddyfile is edited by several projects; anything that is only on a server is one
# deploy away from being lost.
#
# Changing it is a fixed sequence, and `restart` is never part of it: a bad config on restart takes every site
# on the host down, while a failed `reload` leaves the running one in place.
#
#   sudo cp /opt/attendanceqr/Caddyfile /opt/attendanceqr/Caddyfile.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
#   # append the block below
#   docker exec attendanceqr-caddy-1 caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   docker exec attendanceqr-caddy-1 caddy reload   --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# The block is needed even though the file ends in a catch-all: without it, order.qrlog.az is swallowed by that
# catch-all and serves the attendance bundle instead of this application. Caddy routes by specificity, so a
# named block always wins over it.

# BaxOrders (Bakı Abadlıq Xidməti material orders) — separate project, own stack in /opt/baxorders
# (deploy/compose.yml in its repository). Only its web container joins attendanceqr_default, under the alias
# below; its API and PostgreSQL are on the stack's private network and publish no port.
order.qrlog.az {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy baxorders-web:8080
}
